WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.925
Appendix A turns the book into requests.

2
00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:05.662
"Give us commercial
parity" can't be answered.

3
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.206
A specific question tied
to a published rule or a

4
00:00:09.206 --> 00:00:12.662
published exclusion can,
with a yes, a no or a date.

5
00:00:12.662 --> 00:00:16.876
Three habits make a request
hard to deflect: name one item,

6
00:00:16.876 --> 00:00:21.000
cite the basis with a link, and
ask for data, not documents.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.462
Eight requests follow.

8
00:00:22.462 --> 00:00:25.549
Publish the per-feature
exclusion list as data.

9
00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:28.762
Name the exception path
for each excluded feature.

10
00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:34.812
Restore the network observability layer
in GCC, or publish the specific obstacle.

11
00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:37.694
Explain the two same-named GCC packages,

12
00:00:37.694 --> 00:00:41.450
and which exclusions Class
D would and wouldn't lift.

13
00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.500
List the agents and clients that
sit outside the certified offering.

14
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.651
Provide a FedRAMP-compatible trust center
with programmatic access: on October 21,

15
00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:57.713
2026, none of the three Microsoft 365
government packages had a trust-center entry.

16
00:00:57.713 --> 00:01:00.637
Deliver change notices as a JSON feed.

17
00:01:00.637 --> 00:01:04.164
And document the cryptographic
modules for each service,

18
00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:06.000
with their validation status.

19
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.362
Five of the eight rest on obligations the
2026 rules already place on providers,

20
00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:14.125
so none of them needs a boundary argument.

21
00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:15.900
And asking isn't requiring.

22
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.412
Requiring more than FedRAMP
does takes a determination

23
00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.700
by the agency head or a
delegate, and notice to FedRAMP.

24
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.050
The system owner makes these
requests, with acquisition.

25
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.825
Put them to your provider once
your feature inventory exists,

26
00:01:29.825 --> 00:01:34.562
and record each request, its date and
the answer in the authorization package.

27
00:01:34.562 --> 00:01:37.100
A documented no is evidence too.
